BUIILDING RKT LAN:
First goal is to create a LAN network between 3 VMs. 
we have MILO-01 as the server
ANON-01 and ANON-02 as the clients.

I change all network adapters to inter network effectively making a virtual ethernet onnection between all machines.

running ip addr shows the interface exists but none of the device have any ip4 addresses allocated, therefor ip neigh and ip route return nothing.

I need to configure the interface myself by accessing the netplan config.

it currently reads: 
"network:
  version: 2
  ethernets:
    enp0s3:
      dhcp4: true"

which is saying ubuntu needs to use DHCP to obtain IPv4 address. But RKT-LAN doesn't have DHCP server, so instead I'll assign static IP's to all machines.

I'll do this by changing the netplan yaml to:
"network:
  version: 2
  ethernets:
    enp0s3:
      dhcp4: false
      addresses:
        - 10.10.10.10/24"

I'm telling the kernal to stop using DHCP and instead give the enp0s3 interface the address of 10.10.10.10 with a /24 prefix.

I then apply it with sudo netplan apply and test the results with ip addr show enp0s3.

inet being 10.10.10.10/24 on the enp0s3 interface proves the config has been applied properly.

ip route now returns "10.10.10.0/24 dev enp0s3 ... src 10.10.10.10" which shows:
10.10.10.0/24 is the directly connected network
dev enp0s3 shows traffic for that network goes through that interface
src 10.10.10.10 is the source address linux will use.
using the /24 prefix tells linux the address belongs to 10.10.10.0/24
meaning when we set the IP for the other machines I'll use addresses in that range.
Since they'll be in the 10.10.10.0/24 network range linux will know not to use a gateway,
and instead directly route the traffic through the "ethernet" interface.
It can then use ARP to discover who owns 10.10.10.xx.

When I ping 10.10.10.20 currently it returns "Destination Host Unreachable".
and ip neigh returns "10.10.10.20 dev enp0s3 FAILED

So the routing worked fine but when an ARP request was sent and there was no response on the network it returned a FAILED status.

After assigning anon-01 a static ip using ping now returns ICMP packets and ip neigh now shows "10.10.10.20 dev enp0s3 lladdr <MAC> REACHABLE"
10.10.10.20 is anon's IP, dev is the interface used, lladdr is the MAC, and REACHABLE means that linux currently considers the neighbour reachable.

SSH SETUP:
running ssh admin@10.10.10.10 returns "connection refused".
A refusal generally means the connection reached the destination host, but nothing is accepting TCP connections on port 22. Since we know the machines can communicate we know it wont be a routing or ARP issue.
I use systemctl status ssh on MILO-01 to see whether ssh is installed and it returns "ssh.service could not be found". So we know SSH isn't installed on the device.
Using ss -lnt shows nothing is listening on the 22 port.

After creating a new NAT interface for all machines I can now use sudo apt install openssh-server to install the required packages. I realised my netplan config still wasn't configured for the new interface so I changed it to:
"network:
  version: 2
  ethernets:
    enp0s3:
      dhcp4: false
      addresses:
        - 10.10.10.10/24
    enp0s8:
      dhcp4: true"
this allows the NAT interface to simply use DHCP to obtain its IPv4 address.
I then install ssh and systemctl status returns that it's installed just not running 
so I'll run systemctl enable --now which will run ssh and make sure it runs on boot.
systemctl status now shows ssh is active and ss -lnt shows port 22 is open and listening.

ssh admin@10.10.10.10 now works with password authentication.
ssh is currently listening on 0.0.0.0:22 which means any IPv4 on any local interface could technically bruteforce the ssh.

I'll use sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config to change the config to "ListenAddress 10.10.10.10"
once saved and the service is restarted ss -lnt still returns 0.0.0.0:22
Upon doing research the UBUNTU version I am using uses systemd socket activation by default. meaning sshd_config settings are read by a generator that configures ssh.socket.
So my ListenAddress 10.10.10.10 can be valid while ss still shows 0.0.0.0:22 because systemd may currently own the listening socket, rather than sshd binding it directly.
Running systemctl cat ssh.socket shows "ListenStream=0.0.0.0:22" so systemd itself is opening port 22 on all IPv4 and 6 interfaces, then handing the connection to sshd when one arrives. Ubuntu uses socket activation for OpenSSH by default.









